*
why spring boot over spring 

| Feature               | Spring Framework                                  | Spring Boot                                     |
| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| Definition            | Core Java framework for enterprise applications   | Extension of Spring that simplifies development |
| Configuration         | Requires manual configuration (XML / Java Config) | Auto-configuration (minimal setup)              |
| Setup Time            | Slow and complex                                  | Fast and easy                                   |
| Boilerplate Code      | More boilerplate code                             | Very little boilerplate                         |
| Dependency Management | Manage dependencies manually                      | Starter dependencies handle versions            |
| Server                | External server required (Tomcat, etc.)           | Embedded server (Tomcat/Jetty/Undertow)         |
| Deployment            | WAR file                                          | JAR file (standalone app)                       |
| Microservices Support | Possible but complex                              | Ideal for microservices                         |
| Production Features   | Must configure manually                           | Built-in (Actuator, metrics, health checks)     |
| Learning Curve        | Steep                                             | Easy                                            |
| Opinionated Defaults  | No                                                | Yes (but customizable)                          |
| Best Use Case         | Legacy systems, fine-grained control              | Modern apps, REST APIs, microservices           |


1. Less configuration

With Spring:
<bean id="dataSource" class="...">

With Spring Boot:
@SpringBootApplication


That’s often enough to start 🚀

2. Embedded server
Spring → deploy WAR to Tomcat
Spring Boot → Tomcat comes built-in

Just run:
java -jar app.jar

3. Starter dependencies
Instead of managing many libraries:

spring-boot-starter-web
spring-boot-starter-data-jpa
spring-boot-starter-security

Boot picks compatible versions automatically.

4. Production-ready features
Spring Boot includes:
Health checks
Metrics
Logging defaults
Externalized configuration
(All via Spring Boot Actuator)

When would you use plain Spring?

You might choose Spring without Boot if:
You’re maintaining a very old legacy project
You need extreme control over every configuration detail
You’re learning Spring internals deeply
For most modern apps, Boot is preferred.

Simple analogy 🧠

Spring = building a car from parts
Spring Boot = buying a car that’s ready to drive (but you can still customize it)


Spring is a powerful framework for building enterprise Java applications, but it requires a lot of manual configuration and setup.

Spring Boot is built on top of Spring and solves these problems by providing auto-configuration, embedded servers, and starter dependencies, which allow developers to create production-ready applications quickly with minimal configuration.

👉 Spring Boot reduces development time while still using all Spring features.

🧠 One-Line Answer (Very Common Interview Question)

Spring Boot is a convention-over-configuration framework that simplifies Spring application development by providing auto-configuration, embedded servers, and starter dependencies.

🔑 Key Interview Points to Remember

Spring Boot is not a replacement for Spring
Spring Boot uses Spring internally
Boot is preferred for:

REST APIs
Microservices
Rapid development

📝 Quick Memory Trick

Spring → “You configure everything”
Spring Boot → “It configures itself (mostly)”


